  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1219 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nThe primary shortwave exits the region Friday night and this will \nresult in the higher rain chances diminishing at that time. However, \nenough lingering moisture and a final piece or two of energy aloft \ncoming out from the Desert Southwest could result in additional \nisolated to scattered activity Saturday, primarily from the Hill \nCountry through our northeastern most counties. These rain chances \ndo look to dwindle by the evening, yielding to favorable weather for \nTrick-or-Treating. The forecast then trends drier from Sunday into \nthe daytime hours Monday with some warmer afternoon highs up into \nthe mid 80s given the return of greater sunshine with less cloud \ncover. \n\nThe medium range guidance continues to show some differences but are \nstill indicating the potential for a stronger storm system that \ndevelops and advances into the southern and central plains. This \nbrings a cold front to the region sometime overnight into early \nTuesday. The boundary should bring another opportunity for rain and \nstorms to the area. This activity has the potential to be a little \nmore organized with stronger storms with the combination of shear \nand at least some limited instability. Behind the front, expect for \nwinds to shift out of the northwest and the flow will help to usher \nin a drier airmass with cooler lows at least for the region as we \nclose out the forecast period.",
